1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,582
<font color="#FFFFFF">Deep learning depends on huge datasets that are
costly to store, preprocess,</font>

2
00:00:04,662 --> 00:00:08,743
<font color="#FFFFFF">and train on.</font>

3
00:00:08,823 --> 00:00:12,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">Dataset distillation compresses a dataset into a
tiny synthetic one that still trains high-accuracy models.</font>

4
00:00:12,985 --> 00:00:17,066
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces SPEED,</font>

5
00:00:17,146 --> 00:00:21,228
<font color="#FFFFFF">Sparse Parameterization for Epitomic dataset Distillation.</font>

6
00:00:21,308 --> 00:00:25,389
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of tuning the matching objective like most
prior work,</font>

7
00:00:25,469 --> 00:00:29,551
<font color="#FFFFFF">SPEED rethinks how the synthetic dataset itself is
parameterized.</font>

8
00:00:29,631 --> 00:00:33,712
<font color="#FFFFFF">It borrows ideas from dictionary learning and
sparse coding: a shared pool of spatial-agnostic</font>

9
00:00:33,792 --> 00:00:37,874
<font color="#FFFFFF">epitomic tokens acts as a dictionary,</font>

10
00:00:37,954 --> 00:00:42,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">sparse coding matrices pick the significant tokens
per image,</font>

11
00:00:42,115 --> 00:00:46,197
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a feature-recurrent network reassembles them
into high-resolution synthetic images.</font>

12
00:00:46,277 --> 00:00:50,358
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is state-of-the-art distillation,</font>

13
00:00:50,438 --> 00:00:54,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">especially on high-resolution ImageNet subsets,
with a fraction of the storage.</font>

14
00:00:54,900 --> 00:01:02,303
<font color="#FFFFFF">The goal of dataset distillation is to shrink a big
dataset into a small synthetic set that still trains models well.</font>

15
00:01:02,383 --> 00:01:06,397
<font color="#FFFFFF">Most existing work pours its energy into the
matching objective,</font>

16
00:01:06,477 --> 00:01:09,787
<font color="#FFFFFF">the loss that aligns the synthetic and real datasets.</font>

17
00:01:09,867 --> 00:01:14,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">But how the synthetic images are actually
parameterized has been an afterthought.</font>

18
00:01:15,048 --> 00:01:19,253
<font color="#FFFFFF">The standard approach optimizes each synthetic
image independently,</font>

19
00:01:19,333 --> 00:01:26,097
<font color="#FFFFFF">a naive scheme that never exploits the fact that
natural images share enormous amounts of visual structure,</font>

20
00:01:26,177 --> 00:01:29,615
<font color="#FFFFFF">both within a single image and across different images.</font>

21
00:01:29,695 --> 00:01:34,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">This spatial redundancy silently wastes the already
tiny storage budget,</font>

22
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:37,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the problem gets worse as image resolution grows.</font>

23
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:41,324
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key insight is that images are highly redundant.</font>

24
00:01:41,404 --> 00:01:46,641
<font color="#FFFFFF">Patches repeat, textures recur, and similar
structures appear across many images.</font>

25
00:01:46,721 --> 00:01:51,433
<font color="#FFFFFF">Classical representation learning, dictionary
learning and sparse coding,</font>

26
00:01:51,513 --> 00:01:58,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">was built exactly to capture this: represent many
signals as sparse combinations of a shared dictionary.</font>

27
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:04,759
<font color="#FFFFFF">A handful of recent distillation methods started
exploiting relationships between synthetic images,</font>

28
00:02:04,839 --> 00:02:08,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">but none tackled redundancy in a spatially-agnostic way,</font>

29
00:02:08,515 --> 00:02:10,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">no matter where a feature appears.</font>

30
00:02:10,746 --> 00:02:18,084
<font color="#FFFFFF">SPEED asks: what if we spend almost none of the
storage budget on a shared dictionary and per-image sparse codes,</font>

31
00:02:18,164 --> 00:02:21,892
<font color="#FFFFFF">and let a small network reconstruct rich images from them?</font>

32
00:02:21,972 --> 00:02:24,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">That reframing is where the gains come from.</font>

33
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:27,488
<font color="#FFFFFF">SPEED makes three main contributions.</font>

34
00:02:27,568 --> 00:02:31,002
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it introduces spatial-agnostic epitomic tokens,</font>

35
00:02:31,082 --> 00:02:35,428
<font color="#FFFFFF">a shared dictionary of tokens reused by every
synthetic image patch,</font>

36
00:02:35,508 --> 00:02:41,416
<font color="#FFFFFF">together with sparse coding matrices that select
only the most significant tokens per image.</font>

37
00:02:41,496 --> 00:02:44,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it proposes a feature-recurrent network,</font>

38
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:50,657
<font color="#FFFFFF">a compact transformer-style network that
recurrently assembles those tokens into hierarchical,</font>

39
00:02:50,737 --> 00:02:55,864
<font color="#FFFFFF">high-resolution synthetic images while reusing the
same shared tokens and codes.</font>

40
00:02:55,944 --> 00:03:01,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, it shows this parameterization is a drop-in
module: it plugs into gradient,</font>

41
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:06,407
<font color="#FFFFFF">distribution, and trajectory matching objectives
alike and improves all of them.</font>

42
00:03:06,487 --> 00:03:12,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">The framework sets new state-of-the-art results and
is especially strong on high-resolution data.</font>

43
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:17,012
<font color="#FFFFFF">SPEED is tested broadly.</font>

44
00:03:17,092 --> 00:03:21,003
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the standard side,</font>

45
00:03:21,083 --> 00:03:24,995
<font color="#FFFFFF">it uses CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 at thirty-two by
thirty-two resolution and TinyImageNet at sixty-four by sixty-four.</font>

46
00:03:25,075 --> 00:03:28,986
<font color="#FFFFFF">To stress high-resolution performance,</font>

47
00:03:29,066 --> 00:03:32,978
<font color="#FFFFFF">it uses six ImageNet subsets at one-twenty-eight by
one-twenty-eight,</font>

48
00:03:33,058 --> 00:03:36,969
<font color="#FFFFFF">each with ten classes: ImageNette, ImageWoof,
ImageFruit, ImageMeow, ImageSquawk,</font>

49
00:03:37,049 --> 00:03:40,961
<font color="#FFFFFF">and ImageYellow.</font>

50
00:03:41,041 --> 00:03:44,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">Robustness is measured on CIFAR-100-C with fourteen
corruption types at five severity levels.</font>

51
00:03:45,032 --> 00:03:48,944
<font color="#FFFFFF">Everything is compared under equal storage budgets,
counted in parameters per class,</font>

52
00:03:49,024 --> 00:03:52,935
<font color="#FFFFFF">at one, ten, and fifty images per class.</font>

53
00:03:53,015 --> 00:03:56,927
<font color="#FFFFFF">The default backbone is a ConvNet,</font>

54
00:03:57,007 --> 00:04:00,918
<font color="#FFFFFF">the default matching objective is trajectory matching,</font>

55
00:04:00,998 --> 00:04:04,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">and generalization is checked on MLP, ResNet18, and ViT.</font>

56
00:04:05,290 --> 00:04:09,288
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline is a clean sweep.</font>

57
00:04:09,368 --> 00:04:13,367
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across all three standard benchmarks and all six
high-resolution ImageNet subsets,</font>

58
00:04:13,447 --> 00:04:17,445
<font color="#FFFFFF">SPEED sets new state-of-the-art.</font>

59
00:04:17,525 --> 00:04:21,523
<font color="#FFFFFF">At the tightest one-image-per-class budget, it
reaches forty percent on CIFAR-100,</font>

60
00:04:21,603 --> 00:04:25,602
<font color="#FFFFFF">a six point gain, and twenty-six point nine percent
on TinyImageNet,</font>

61
00:04:25,682 --> 00:04:29,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">a ten point nine gain over the previous best.</font>

62
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:33,758
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the ImageNet subsets it averages an eleven point
two percent improvement at the same budget.</font>

63
00:04:33,838 --> 00:04:37,837
<font color="#FFFFFF">Strikingly,</font>

64
00:04:37,917 --> 00:04:41,915
<font color="#FFFFFF">its one-image-per-class results match what prior
methods needed ten images per class to achieve,</font>

65
00:04:41,995 --> 00:04:45,993
<font color="#FFFFFF">using only about ten percent of their storage.</font>

66
00:04:46,073 --> 00:04:50,072
<font color="#FFFFFF">It also stays best at every step of continual learning,</font>

67
00:04:50,152 --> 00:04:54,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">and under corruption on ResNet18 it nearly doubles
the accuracy of prior methods.</font>

68
00:04:54,530 --> 00:04:58,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablations show sparsification is essentially free.</font>

69
00:04:58,895 --> 00:05:03,181
<font color="#FFFFFF">Pruning each sparse coding matrix to just
forty-eight non-zero elements,</font>

70
00:05:03,261 --> 00:05:07,546
<font color="#FFFFFF">about three hundred thousand parameters,</font>

71
00:05:07,626 --> 00:05:11,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">meets the storage budget while keeping ConvNet
accuracy at seventy-three point five percent,</font>

72
00:05:11,992 --> 00:05:16,277
<font color="#FFFFFF">barely below the seventy-four percent of the full
fifteen-million-parameter model.</font>

73
00:05:16,357 --> 00:05:20,643
<font color="#FFFFFF">Push k too low, down to twelve, and accuracy collapses,</font>

74
00:05:20,723 --> 00:05:25,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a moderate value is best and even improves
cross-architecture generalization.</font>

75
00:05:25,088 --> 00:05:29,374
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the network side, two recurrent blocks and three
heads work best.</font>

76
00:05:29,454 --> 00:05:33,739
<font color="#FFFFFF">And qualitatively,</font>

77
00:05:33,819 --> 00:05:38,105
<font color="#FFFFFF">synthetic images before and after sparsification to
zero point three percent density look almost identical,</font>

78
00:05:38,185 --> 00:05:42,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming that the pruned features preserve the
global semantics.</font>

79
00:05:42,850 --> 00:05:46,994
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message of SPEED is that
parameterization deserves as much attention as the matching objective.</font>

80
00:05:47,074 --> 00:05:51,218
<font color="#FFFFFF">By treating a synthetic dataset as a shared
dictionary of spatial-agnostic epitomic tokens,</font>

81
00:05:51,298 --> 00:05:55,442
<font color="#FFFFFF">sparse per-image coding matrices,</font>

82
00:05:55,522 --> 00:05:59,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a small recurrent network that reassembles them,</font>

83
00:05:59,746 --> 00:06:03,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">SPEED removes the spatial redundancy that naive
methods leave on the table.</font>

84
00:06:03,970 --> 00:06:08,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">The payoff is state-of-the-art distillation at a
fraction of the storage,</font>

85
00:06:08,194 --> 00:06:12,338
<font color="#FFFFFF">biggest on high-resolution images,</font>

86
00:06:12,418 --> 00:06:16,562
<font color="#FFFFFF">plus better generalization to unseen architectures
and stronger robustness to corruption.</font>

87
00:06:16,642 --> 00:06:20,786
<font color="#FFFFFF">In short, sparse,</font>

88
00:06:20,866 --> 00:06:25,010
<font color="#FFFFFF">shared representation is a powerful and general
lever for making tiny synthetic datasets do far more.</font>
